{"banned_sql": ["INSERT OR REPLACE", "INSERT OR IGNORE", "UPDATE", "DELETE"], "banned_sql_reason": "OR IGNORE silently DROPS a divergent incoming record and reports success; OR REPLACE silently DESTROYS stored evidence. Both are present elsewhere in the repo for mutable cached state and would be copied by reflex. Evidence is never mutated, so the module contains no UPDATE and no DELETE at all.", "decision": "stdlib sqlite3", "durability_settings": ["journal_mode=WAL", "synchronous=FULL", "busy_timeout=5000", "BEGIN IMMEDIATE for put"], "external_service_required": false, "kind": "ArchitectureDecision", "mission_id": "northstar_0c_pit_evidence_gateway_research_store", "paid_vendor_required": false, "payload_column_reason": "SQLite type affinity on a TEXT column would coerce values and could flip 100 <-> 100.0, which is identity-bearing in canonical JSON and would mint a different snapshot. BLOB stores the exact to_json() bytes and refuses a non-bytes value outright.", "payload_column_type": "BLOB", "rationale": "Decisive and structural, not stylistic. EvidenceSnapshot identity deliberately EXCLUDES acquisition metadata (retrieved_at is dropped from the identity view; provenance.recorded_at is excluded), so one snapshot_id legitimately corresponds to more than one canonical byte string. A content-addressed store keys on hash(bytes), so those two byte strings land at two different addresses and the identity conflict becomes INVISIBLE -- the store cannot express the very condition RS2 requires it to fail closed on. Keying by snapshot_id instead means abandoning content addressing and hand-rolling a key-value store plus an index, i.e. reimplementing SQLite. Separately, a snapshot write is not one fact (row + indexes + later audit rows) and needs multi-row atomicity, which a blob+index pair cannot provide: an index entry can name a blob that was never fsynced. The repository already treats SQLite as the convention for AUTHORITATIVE state (rd_control/registry.py, holdings_store.py, capability_store.py); the content-addressed review_packet_store is the deliberate exception because it stores preimages of digests recorded authoritatively elsewhere.", "recorded_at": "2026-08-17T03:11:54.195409+00:00", "rejected": "content-addressed file store", "server_required": false, "session_id": "ns0c-research-store-foundation-003", "stdlib_only": true, "store_is_git_tracked": false, "store_tracking_reason": "The store is a runtime artifact, not certification evidence. Keeping it untracked also avoids the WAL-sidecar hazard: after a commit the main .db can still be a bare header page, so copying it alone yields no such table. Any future export must PRAGMA wal_checkpoint(TRUNCATE) or use Connection.backup(). Tests are hermetic under tmp_path.", "task_id": "ns0c-rs-t1"}
